A [marquise diamond necklace](/marquise-cut-diamond) is the one pendant where stone orientation matters as much as size. Turn a marquise east-west on a chain and you have a bold, modern pendant. Keep it pointing up and you have a classic elongating solitaire. Both work. Most buyers never know this is a choice they're making.

The same shape also changes which chain lengths work on your body. A round diamond pendant looks fine at any length. A marquise pendant has a sweet spot. Get it right and the stone looks 20% larger than its carat weight. Get it wrong and it disappears into your neckline.

This guide covers orientation, chain length, and setting protection. Lab stone pricing is in here too — bigger stone, same budget. All prices are current certified market inventory from live listings.

## What Makes a Marquise Diamond Necklace Different

The marquise cut has 58 facets — the same as a round brilliant. It returns light just as well. But the elongated body scatters light in a directional pattern. Longer flashes along the length. Wider flashes at the center belly.

In a pendant, that directionality works in your favor. A marquise oriented point-up creates a vertical flash that draws the eye down the stone's length. That elongates the perceived distance from your chin to your collarbone. A round brilliant scatters light equally in all directions. It sparkles. It doesn't elongate.

This shape also has a larger face-up surface area than a round at the same carat weight. A 0.5ct marquise looks visually similar to a 0.65ct round on the chest. That size-per-dollar advantage is significant when you're comparing pendants across price ranges.

The bezel setting is the standard choice for marquise pendants, not a premium option. The pointed tips of a marquise are its most stress-concentrated points. In a ring, V-prongs protect them. In a pendant, a bezel wraps both tips in metal. It absorbs impact rather than passing it to the stone. Every product in this guide uses a bezel setting.

| Feature | Marquise Pendant | Round Pendant | What this means |
|---------|-----------------|---------------|-----------------|
| Face-up size at 0.5ct | ~8mm × 4mm | ~5.2mm diameter | Marquise looks ~25% larger at the same carat weight |
| Light pattern | Directional, elongating | Omni-directional | Marquise flash pulls the eye vertically, flattering the neck |
| Setting needed | Bezel (tip protection) | Prong or bezel | Marquise requires more protective setting for daily wear |
| Best chain length | 18" (sweet spot) | 16"–20" (any) | Marquise needs vertical breathing room below the collarbone |

**Verdict:** For buyers who want maximum visual presence per dollar, the marquise pendant beats the round at equivalent carat weights. The directional flash and larger face-up area are genuine advantages — not marketing language.

## The Orient Effect

The Orient Effect is what happens when you rotate a marquise diamond 90 degrees on its pendant bail.

Most buyers receive a pendant with the stone pointing up (vertical). That's the traditional position. The long axis runs parallel to the chain. The bottom point aims at the chest. The elongating flash runs top to bottom — creating the classic vertical pendant look: elegant, slimming, traditional.

East-west means the stone sits perpendicular to the chain. The wide axis of the marquise now spans the horizontal. The stone reads as breadth rather than length. This is the modern, architectural look. It draws the eye across the chest rather than down the neck. Bold, fashion-forward, and better for low or open necklines where horizontal visual weight complements rather than competes.

[![Marquise diamond 0.25ct yellow gold pendant in bezel setting](/images/marquise-diamond-necklace-quarter-carat-yellow-gold.jpg)](https://www.bluenile.com/james-allen/item-311061?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=Image)

| Orientation | Body Effect | Best Neckline | Chain Length | What this means |
|-------------|------------|---------------|--------------|-----------------|
| Point-up (vertical) | Elongates neck visually | Round, crew, square | 18" | The stone's length creates downward visual movement — adds perceived height to the neck |
| East-west (horizontal) | Broadens chest emphasis | V-neck, off-shoulder | 20" | Stone width fills horizontal space — bolder look without buying a larger stone |

**Verdict:** If you're buying one pendant for everyday wear, choose point-up orientation at 18". It works with more outfits. It creates the elongating effect that makes a marquise pendant different from a round or oval. If you want a fashion piece for specific occasion outfits, east-west at 20" on a deep V-neck is genuinely striking.

Most pendant listings default to point-up. If you want east-west, confirm the bail can rotate before ordering. Some bails have a fixed orientation.

### Point-up: When It Flatters Most

A vertical marquise pendant works on almost every body type and neckline combination. The elongating flash makes the neck look longer. It works in winter with turtlenecks (the pendant is fully visible above the neck). It works in summer on round necklines. It works under a blazer because the chain hangs cleanly without the stone jutting sideways.

The one exception: a very deep V-neck where the stone's tip disappears into the fabric. At that depth, east-west reads better. The stone anchors in the V rather than getting swallowed by it.

### East-West: When It Works Best

An east-west marquise pendant is a deliberate style choice. You wear it when you want a horizontal anchor at the chest, not a vertical drop. A 20" chain on a V-neck dress, stone sitting horizontal in the V, creates a finished, intentional look.

It also works on a plain crew neck. The horizontal orientation breaks the vertical symmetry of the outfit. One stone, maximum interest.

