**Who 20" is right for:** Buyers who wear V-necks regularly, buyers who want to layer with a shorter collarbone chain, buyers with a shorter neck who find 18" chains land at or above the collarbone.

## Bezel vs Prong for Marquise Pendants

Every pendant in this guide uses a bezel setting. Here is why that is the right choice for a marquise specifically.

A marquise diamond has two pointed tips. In a [marquise ring](/marquise-diamond-engagement-ring), V-prongs cap each end — one at the top, one at the bottom. They protect the tips from lateral impact. In a pendant, V-prong protection at the top tip conflicts with the bail attachment. The bail connects to the pendant mount right where the upper V-prong would sit. This leaves the top tip exposed.

A bezel solves this cleanly. A bezel is a continuous metal collar. It wraps the entire girdle — top tip, sides, bottom tip. The bail attaches above the bezel, not at the stone's tip. The result is complete tip protection without compromising the bail attachment.

| Setting | Protection | Brilliance | Style | Durability | What this means |
|---------|------------|------------|-------|------------|-----------------|
| Full bezel | Complete; both tips and girdle covered | 5–10% less light exposure | Modern, streamlined | Excellent for daily wear | The right choice for any marquise worn regularly |
| 4-prong with V-tips | Tips capped, girdle exposed | Maximum light return | Vintage, open | Tips can snag fabric or loosen in pendant over years | Higher risk in a pendant context; better in a ring |
| 4-prong (no V-tips) | Body only; tips fully exposed | Maximum light return | Traditional | Tips unprotected — not appropriate for marquise pendant | Do not choose this for a marquise pendant |

**Verdict:** Bezel is the right setting for a marquise pendant. The reduction in light exposure (5–10%) is not visible to the naked eye under everyday lighting. The tip protection is real and permanent. This is not a compromise — it's the technically correct choice for this shape in this setting type.

## Metal Choice for Marquise Pendants

White gold and yellow gold are identically priced at every carat weight in this guide. The choice is purely visual.

White gold makes the diamond appear to float. The metal recedes and the stone reads as the only element. This works with any stone color grade from D to J. It gives a contemporary, clean look that works with silver jewelry, platinum watches, and cool-toned outfits.

Yellow gold sets the diamond against a warm contrast. For H–J near-colorless stones, yellow gold is a strategic choice. It makes slight warmth in the stone look intentional and rich. That reads better than a grading compromise. Yellow gold also works better with warm-toned skin and pairs naturally with rose gold.

Platinum is more expensive and more durable. Unlike 14k white gold, platinum does not require rhodium replating over time. It starts white and stays white. The premium ranges from $130 (0.25ct) to over $1,000 (1ct). For a pendant you wear every day for decades, the platinum premium may be worth it.

| Metal | Best Stone Color | Visual Effect | Price Premium | What this means |
|-------|-----------------|---------------|---------------|-----------------|
| 14k White Gold | D–J (any) | Stone floats; modern | Baseline | Most versatile; works with any color grade and any outfit |
| 14k Yellow Gold | H–J (warm) | Warm, vintage, bold | None (same price) | Smart for near-colorless stones — masks slight warmth beautifully |
| Platinum | D–G (ideal) | Bright, luxurious | $130–$1,100+ | Best long-term durability; no replating ever required |

**Verdict:** 14k white gold is the right default. Same price as yellow gold, works with any stone color, maximum outfit flexibility. Choose yellow gold if you wear mostly yellow gold jewelry or if your stone is H–J grade. Choose platinum if you want the pendant to last 30+ years without metal maintenance.

## Toi et Moi Marquise Necklaces

A Toi et Moi pendant pairs two stones in one setting. The name means "you and me" in French. It traditionally symbolizes two people, two relationships, or two moments. On a necklace, the two stones sit side by side in a shared mount.

These lab-grown pendants pair a round brilliant with a marquise diamond. The round provides symmetrical scintillation. The marquise provides directional elongation. The contrast between a curved shape and a pointed shape is what makes this style visually arresting. Neither stone overwhelms the other — the two read as a unit.

[![Marquise diamond toi et moi necklace in 14k rose gold with round brilliant diamond](/images/marquise-diamond-necklace-toi-et-moi-rose-gold.jpg)](https://www.bluenile.com/james-allen/item-316294?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=Image)

All three metal versions are $2,535:

- [14k White Gold Toi et Moi Lab Pendant — $2,535](https://www.bluenile.com/james-allen/item-316292?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational)
- [14k Yellow Gold Toi et Moi Lab Pendant — $2,535](https://www.bluenile.com/james-allen/item-316293?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational)
- [14k Rose Gold Toi et Moi Lab Pendant — $2,535](https://www.bluenile.com/james-allen/item-316294?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational)

The rose gold version adds a third visual relationship. Warm pink metal, white brilliance from the round stone, and the marquise catching directional light along its length. It's a strong milestone anniversary gift. It also works as a self-purchase for anyone who wants more visual interest than a standard solitaire.

Chain length for a Toi et Moi pendant: 18" for daily wear. Choose 20" if the setting is wide and you want more visual space below it.

